<h1>Carriers and Wholesalers in Renewals</h1>

<p>
    Understanding the relationship between carriers and wholesalers is key to managing renewals efficiently. This section explains how these relationships work and why they matter.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Diagram showing the relationship between carriers, wholesalers, and renewals. Use boxes and arrows to illustrate: Wholesaler → Carrier → Renewal. Include a brief explanation of the flow. -->

<h2>What's the Difference?</h2>

<p>
    <strong>Carriers</strong> are insurance companies that actually issue policies (like State Farm, Travelers, or Progressive). <strong>Wholesalers</strong> are brokers or agencies that help you access carriers you might not have a direct relationship with.
</p>

<p>
    Think of it this way: if you need to get a quote from a carrier but don't have direct access, you might go through a wholesaler who does have that relationship. The wholesaler acts as a middleman to help you get quotes from carriers.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Simple illustration showing a direct appointment (Agency → Carrier) vs. a wholesaler relationship (Agency → Wholesaler → Carrier). Use different colors or styles to distinguish the paths. -->

<h2>Why This Matters for Renewals</h2>

<p>
    When you set up carriers and wholesalers correctly in Quickfire, several things happen automatically:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Faster submissions</strong> – When creating a submission, the system shows you the right carriers and wholesalers based on your relationships</li>
    <li><strong>Better organization</strong> – You can see at a glance which wholesaler can help you access which carriers</li>
    <li><strong>Contact information</strong> – Quick access to the right people at carriers and wholesalers for loss runs, submissions, and questions</li>
    <li><strong>Portal links</strong> – Quick links to carrier and wholesaler portals for submitting applications and checking status</li>
</ul>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot of the carrier edit page showing fields for wholesaler assignment, portal URLs, loss run emails, and submission contacts. Highlight the key fields with callouts. -->

<h2>Setting Up Carrier-Wholesaler Relationships</h2>

<p>
    To link a carrier to a wholesaler:
</p>

<ol>
    <li>Go to the <strong>Carriers</strong> section</li>
    <li>Find the carrier you want to edit and click <strong>Edit</strong></li>
    <li>In the carrier details, you'll see options to assign wholesalers</li>
    <li>Select which wholesalers have access to this carrier</li>
    <li>Save your changes</li>
</ol>

<p>
    Once this relationship is set up, when you create a renewal or submission, the system will know which wholesalers can help you access that carrier.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Step-by-step screenshots showing: 1) Carriers list page, 2) Edit button clicked, 3) Wholesaler assignment section, 4) Dropdown showing available wholesalers. Use numbered annotations. -->

<h2>Storing Important Information</h2>

<p>
    Each carrier and wholesaler record can store valuable information that makes your work easier:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Portal URLs</strong> – Direct links to their online portals for submissions, quotes, and policy management</li>
    <li><strong>Loss Run Emails</strong> – The email address where you send requests for loss runs</li>
    <li><strong>Submission URLs</strong> – Direct links to submission portals or forms</li>
    <li><strong>Contact Information</strong> – Names, emails, and phone numbers for key contacts</li>
    <li><strong>Notes</strong> – Important reminders about appetites, requirements, or special instructions</li>
</ul>

<p>
    This information appears automatically when you're working on renewals, so you don't have to hunt for it each time.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot of a carrier detail page showing all the information fields mentioned above, with labels or callouts explaining what each field is used for. -->

<h2>Using Carrier Information in Renewals</h2>

<p>
    When you're working on a renewal, the carrier and wholesaler information you've set up helps in several ways:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Creating submissions</strong> – The submission form will show you the right carriers and wholesalers based on your relationships</li>
    <li><strong>Finding contacts</strong> – When you need to reach out about a quote or submission, the right contact information is right there</li>
    <li><strong>Accessing portals</strong> – Quick links to portals mean less time searching for the right website</li>
    <li><strong>Tracking history</strong> – See which carriers and wholesalers you've worked with on past renewals</li>
</ul>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot of a submission creation dialog showing the carrier and wholesaler dropdowns, with an annotation explaining that these options are filtered based on your relationships. -->

<h2>Keeping Information Current</h2>

<p>
    Carrier and wholesaler information changes over time—contacts move, portals get updated, and relationships change. Here are some tips for keeping things current:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Update contacts regularly</strong> – When someone tells you a contact has changed, update it right away</li>
    <li><strong>Review relationships periodically</strong> – Check that your carrier-wholesaler relationships are still accurate</li>
    <li><strong>Add notes</strong> – If a carrier changes their appetite or requirements, add a note so your team knows</li>
    <li><strong>Use the policy dropdowns</strong> – When working on policies, use the contact dropdowns to keep loss run and submission contacts up to date</li>
</ul>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot showing the contact management section within a carrier record, with an example of how to add or update a contact. Show the "Add Contact" button and form fields. -->

<h2>Best Practices</h2>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Set up relationships early</strong> – Don't wait until you need them. Set up carrier-wholesaler relationships when you first add them to the system</li>
    <li><strong>Be specific with notes</strong> – Include details like "Requires 5 years of loss runs" or "Best for contractors" in carrier notes</li>
    <li><strong>Keep portal links current</strong> – Update URLs when carriers change their websites</li>
    <li><strong>Use consistent naming</strong> – Make sure carrier and wholesaler names are consistent so they're easy to find</li>
</ul>

